1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,225
Every design meeting needs a diagram.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,363
Twenty minutes of boxes and arrows.

3
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:07,129
Five minutes of explaining.

4
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:09,927
Draft Canvas was built to flip that.

5
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:13,534
Sketch a service and a data store.

6
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:15,564
Every shape has a real type.

7
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:17,701
This store is SQL.

8
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:21,212
Add a queue, and the arrows label
themselves.

9
00:00:24,863 --> 00:00:25,951
Draw it yourself…

10
00:00:26,263 --> 00:00:28,418
or hand the pencil to your coding agent.

11
00:00:29,663 --> 00:00:33,204
It reads your code and draws the
architecture, pass by pass.

12
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:37,945
Ask for a cleaner layout — it fixes that
too.

13
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:42,804
Now, the part that matters: explaining it.

14
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:47,822
Start with the whole system, then step
inside any service, down to its components.

15
00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:51,783
Then present the flow, one step at a time.

16
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:54,968
So the whole room follows the same request.

17
00:00:58,530 --> 00:01:00,173
Work on it together, live.

18
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:04,156
Every change lands on your copy.

19
00:01:04,930 --> 00:01:09,431
And take it anywhere: flows to Mermaid,
architecture to draw.io.

20
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:11,560
No account.

21
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:12,753
No tracking.

22
00:01:13,350 --> 00:01:14,630
Saved on your device.

23
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:16,751
Free and open source.

24
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:23,566
So, the next design meeting: five minutes of
boxes and arrows — and twenty of explaining!

25
00:01:24,533 --> 00:01:26,048
Draw less. Explain more.
